          "turn": 6,
          "prompt": "Explain how src/inventory/reorder.py decides what to order.",
          "answer": "Based on the code in `src/inventory/reorder.py`, the reorder system works as follows:\n\n## How it decides what to order:\n\n### 1. What items are flagged for reorder (`reorder_due()` function):\n- Returns a list of SKUs that are at or below the `REORDER_THRESHOLD` (set to 12 in config)\n- This identifies which items currently have insufficient inventory\n\n### 2. How much to order (`order_size()` function):\n- Calculates the shortfall as: `REORDER_THRESHOLD * 3 - level_for(sku)`\n- This means it aims to bring each item up to 3 times the reorder threshold (36 units for the default threshold of 12)\n- The result is capped at `MAX_BATCH` (set to 250 in config) \n- If the current level is already above the target, it returns 0 (no order needed)\n\n## Summary:\nThe system automatically identifies items running low (at or below 12 units) and calculates how many additional units to order to bring them up to 36 units total, with a maximum batch size limit of 250 units. This approach ensures that inventory is replenished to a sufficient level while avoiding overly large orders.",
